--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` refuses the unhyphenated spelling of a `Core\Uuid` argument, because the argument matcher takes exactly what `Core\Uuid::parse` takes — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`, spec § 11
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound stated on both sides: the sibling case's canonical word converts,
// and the same sixteen octets written without hyphens do not. That refusal is
// not the matcher being strict on its own account — it is
// `nvs_runtime::uuid::read` being the *only* reader, and
// `uuid-parses-only-the-canonical-form.nvst` pins the same three rejected
// spellings for `Core\Uuid::parse`. A matcher that accepted a second spelling
// here would be a second grammar, and one of the two would drift.
//
// A usage error rather than a throw, for the reason the `decimal` sibling
// states: a command line is input.
class Fleet {
    #[Core\Command(name: "show", about: "Show one account")]
    public static function show(Core\Uuid $account): uint {
        echo "show ran, and no word on this command line converted\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

echo "status: ", Core\Command::run(), "\n";
echo "still running", "\n";
--ARGS--
show
f9168c5eceb24faab6bf329bf39fa1e4
--EXPECT--
status: 2
still running
